Keep the emulator out of App Nap while a guest runs.

macOS naps an application whose windows are not in front: its timers and
sleeps are coalesced, by up to about a second. The emulator is such an
application whenever its window is behind another, and everything a guest asks
of it then waits: venus's renderer polls its command rings with short sleeps,
and the guest's Vulkan driver waits on each reply, so one synchronous Vulkan
call took about a second. An application launched in the guest with the
window behind took 5.6 s to show its first frame, and 0.7 s with the window in
front.

The application controller begins a user-initiated, latency-critical activity
once it has launched, and holds it for the life of the process. It still lets
the machine sleep when idle.

--- a/ui/cocoa.m	2026-10-19 06:41:47
+++ b/ui/cocoa.m	2026-10-19 06:41:56
@@ -1340,6 +1340,17 @@
 {
     COCOA_DEBUG("QemuCocoaAppController: applicationDidFinishLaunching\n");
     allow_events = true;
+
+    /*
+     * A guest keeps time and answers its devices whether or not this window
+     * is in front. Without an activity macOS naps the process when it is
+     * not, coalescing its timers and sleeps by up to a second, and every
+     * device that polls or sleeps between polls answers that much later.
+     */
+    [[[NSProcessInfo processInfo]
+        beginActivityWithOptions:NSActivityUserInitiatedAllowingIdleSystemSleep |
+                                 NSActivityLatencyCritical
+                          reason:@"A virtual machine is running"] retain];
 }
 
 - (void)applicationWillTerminate:(NSNotification *)aNotification
